# Cinakey — Source of Truth

## 1. Product
Cinakey is the studio pipeline for AI filmmaking: one browser workspace that takes a project from idea to exported cut. Competitors (LTX Studio, Google Flow, Higgsfield) sell access to models; Cinakey wins on pipeline depth: the film is structured, versioned data (script, shots, takes, assets, edit decisions) that the AI copilot can reason over and that can be exported cleanly.
Users: solo creators first (YouTubers, indie filmmakers, ad and social makers); studios and agencies later (phase 3). Core job: "Turn my idea into a finished, consistent video without juggling ten tools or losing track of what worked."
North-star metric: finished minutes exported per active workspace per month.
Built by a solo developer using AI-assisted coding.

## 2. Principles
1. Pipeline over model count: one model per job at launch (Seedance 2.5 video, GPT Image 2 images, DeepSeek copilot) behind model-neutral adapters so more can be added without product changes.
2. Everything is a linked record: every asset knows its project, scene, shot and the job, prompt and references that made it.
3. Nothing is lost: scripts, sheets, shots, blockouts and timelines are versioned with parent pointers; users compare, branch and roll back.
4. The AI works on the project: the copilot reads project data and proposes changes as diffs with cost estimates; the user accepts or rejects.
5. Open at the edges: import any media; export MP4 and JSON (later OTIO/XML for Premiere, Resolve, Final Cut).

## 3. Tech stack and hard rules
- Front end: React + TypeScript (strict), Vite, hosted as a static site (Vercel, Netlify or Cloudflare Pages).
- Back end: Convex (database, queries, mutations, actions, scheduled functions, HTTP actions, file storage, text and vector search). No separate API server.
- Repository: npm workspaces monorepo (`apps/*`, `packages/*`) with Turborepo. `apps/web` is the creator app (`@cinakey/web`); `packages/backend` is the Convex project (`@cinakey/backend`); `packages/shared` holds cross-cutting types (`@cinakey/shared`); `packages/config` holds shared tsconfig / ESLint / Prettier (`@cinakey/config`). Later: `apps/admin` and `packages/ui` (not created yet).
- Auth: Convex Auth (email and password, magic links, Google OAuth). Staff accounts are flagged separately from customers.
- Secrets: provider keys (SEEDANCE_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, DEEPSEEK_API_KEY, etc.) are Convex environment variables, read via process.env only inside actions. Nothing secret in VITE_ variables or the repo.
- Providers: Seedance 2.5 via ByteDance's base API (video, upscale, extend); GPT Image 2 (images, edits); DeepSeek (copilot LLM).
- Storage: Convex file storage behind ONE storage module at `packages/backend/convex/storage.ts` (and matching helpers). Nothing else touches storage directly, so video can later move to Cloudflare R2 (free egress) by changing that module only.
- Convex documents are limited to 1 MiB: large JSON (blockout scenes, Theatre.js state, timeline snapshots) is stored as files; the database row holds the file ID.
- Rendering happens in the browser: WebCodecs for encode/decode, WebGL/WebGPU for compositing, low-res proxies for playback. No render server in the MVP.
- 3D blockout: three.js (scene, mannequins, props, lights, cameras) + Theatre.js (keyframed animation per shot).
- Real-time comes from Convex live queries (job status, notifications, later presence and shot locks). Yjs for co-editing in phase 3.

## 4. Pipeline
Brief -> Script -> Look development -> Blockout -> Shot generation -> Edit -> Review -> Export. Each stage's output is the next stage's structured input; users can enter at any stage (e.g. upload footage and start at Edit). Review notes loop back to single shots (regenerate or swap a take).

## 5. Modules and features
MVP items are marked [MVP]; others note their phase.
1. Script room [MVP]: screenplay and A/V formats; scenes, beats and dialogue lines as records; runtime estimate; entity extraction (characters, locations, props) into look-dev cards; script versions with diff; shots flagged out of date when their lines change.
2. Look development [MVP]: character, location, prop and style sheets; locked identity references; GPT Image 2 generation and edits inside sheets; import real photos as references.
3. Blockout [MVP]: shot list table (type, lens, move, duration, characters, location, dialogue, status); storyboard with one keyframe per shot; 3D blockout (three.js + Theatre.js) with mannequins, props, lights and an animated camera; renders usable as depth/pose guides for generation; export one shot, a scene or all as JSON and/or MP4 (animatic with optional burn-in of shot number, scene and timecode). Animatic timeline is phase 2.
4. Shot generation [MVP]: prompts assembled from shot, character, location and style data; multiple takes per shot, one selected; start/end frames, references and camera presets as far as Seedance 2.5 supports (controls come from the adapter's capability manifest); upscale and extend via Seedance; fixing = regenerate as a new take or trim in the editor; every take records model and version. Batch scene generation and lip sync are phase 2.
5. Browser editor [MVP, in-house]: multi-track timeline (video, dialogue, music, SFX, titles); cut, trim, split, reorder; uploaded and generated clips side by side; swap take / regenerate from the timeline; music upload, captions; in-browser MP4 export. Aspect-ratio variants, colour/LUTs and OTIO/XML export are phase 2.
6. Asset library [MVP]: upload footage, photos, audio, music and logos (MIME + size validation via the storage module); typed assets linked to project/scene/shot/job with display `name` and searchable tags; grid and list views; filters by type, tag, character (via shot), shot; text search (Convex search index on name+tags); detail drawer with preview, metadata, tags, and lineage (job, model, prompt, seed, parents); star, rename, tag, delete with confirm; per-project and cross-project library. Selected takes kept; rejected takes purged after a set period unless starred (purge deferred).
7. AI copilot [MVP]: DeepSeek via its API behind an adapter; chat panel aware of current view, selection and project; primed as film director, screenwriter and character designer; acts through tools (edit script, create shots, write prompts, queue generations) shown as proposals with cost estimates; project rules list it follows (e.g. "no handheld shots"). Workspace reserves a right-side copilot panel slot (empty until this module ships).
8. Platform essentials: cost estimates, batch confirmation, spend caps [MVP]; in-app and email job notifications, automatic retries and credit refunds [MVP]; guided starter template [MVP]; internal admin via Convex dashboard plus protected staff pages [MVP]; share links (phase 2); project members with Owner/Editor/Commenter/Viewer roles and shot locks (phase 2). Projects dashboard (create from brief, rename, shallow duplicate, soft-archive) and project workspace with pipeline stage tabs + derived progress [MVP].
9. Admin platform (phase 2): separate React app in `apps/admin` on the same Convex deployment; areas for users, projects, jobs/providers, billing/credits, moderation, models/pricing, analytics, admin users, audit log; RBAC roles (Super admin, Operations admin, Support, Moderator, Finance, Engineer, Analyst) built from fine-grained permissions; every admin function checks permissions server-side; append-only audit log.

## 6. Data model (Convex tables)
MVP ownership: each user has a personal `workspace`; every `project` belongs to a workspace; access = workspace owner (or `users.isStaff`). `projectMembers` / share links / admin RBAC come later.

Versioning: `scriptVersions` and `timelineVersions` insert new rows with optional `parentId` (never overwrite content in place). Blockout JSON is a new storage blob; `shots.blockoutFileId` points at the current file. Live working records (`scenes`, `shots` metadata) may be patched. Large JSON always lives in file storage; documents hold only storage IDs (`sizeBytes` required on assets). Credits are per workspace (`creditLedger` append-only). Soft-archive projects via `archivedAt` (no hard delete in MVP).

| Table | Key fields | Indexes |
|-------|------------|---------|
| `users` (extends Auth) | auth fields + `isStaff?`, `personalWorkspaceId?` | `email`, `phone` |
| `workspaces` | `name`, `ownerUserId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` | `by_owner` |
| `projects` | `workspaceId`, `title`, `brief?` `{logline, audience?, tone?}`, `aspectRatio`, `fps`, `targetLengthSec?`, `styleNotes?`, `rules[]`, `thumbnailAssetId?`, `archivedAt?`, timestamps | `by_workspace` |
| `scriptVersions` | `projectId`, `parentId?`, `format` (screenplay\|av), `label?`, `contentFileId`, `createdBy`, `createdAt` | `by_project`, `by_parent` |
| `scenes` | `projectId`, `order`, `heading`, `synopsis?`, timestamps | `by_project`, `by_project_order` |
| `shots` | `projectId`, `sceneId`, `order`, `shotType`, `lensMm?`, `cameraMove?`, `durationSec`, `characterIds[]`, `locationId?`, `dialogue?`, `status`, `selectedTakeId?`, `blockoutFileId?`, `notes?`, timestamps | `by_project`, `by_scene`, `by_scene_order` |
| `entities` | `projectId`, `kind` (character\|location\|prop\|style), `name`, `description?`, `lockedReferenceAssetIds[]`, `sheetFileId?`, timestamps | `by_project`, `by_project_kind` |
| `assets` | `projectId`, `sceneId?`, `shotId?`, `jobId?`, `type`, `name`, `searchText`, `storageId`, `format`, `sizeBytes`, `durationSec?`, `width?`/`height?`, `tags[]`, `lineage?`, `starred`, `createdBy`, timestamps | `by_project`, `by_project_type`, `by_shot`, `by_job`, `by_storage`; search `search_name_tags` on `searchText` (filter: projectId, type, starred) |
| `generationJobs` | `projectId`, `shotId?`, `model`, `modelVersion`, `kind` (operation), `prompt`, `seed?`, `inputsFileId?`, `estimatedCostCredits`, `actualCostCredits?`, `status` (queued→submitted→running→succeeded\|failed\|refunded), `providerJobId?`, `outputAssetIds[]`, `errorMessage?`, `attempts`, `createdBy`, timestamps | `by_project`, `by_status`, `by_provider_job` |
